  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function h(x) that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. The current construction achieves C\u2085 \u2264 0.38085, but we aim for a tighter bound of 0.3807.\n\nTo improve upon the existing gradient descent approach, we propose the following strategy:\n\n- **Higher Resolution Discretization**: Increase n_points from 100 to 200 to better capture the function's shape.\n- **Adaptive Learning Rate**: Use the Adam optimizer instead of fixed learning rate gradient descent.\n- **Simulated Annealing**: Periodically perturb the solution to escape local minima.\n- **Efficient Constraint Handling**: Use a projection operator to maintain 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and exact integral constraint.\n- **Precomputation**: Precompute correlation lags to speed up evaluation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet h : [0,2] \u2192 [0,1], discretized into n_points samples, such that:\n\n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1 \u2192 \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- C\u2085 = max_k [\u2211h[i](1 - h[i+k]))] * dx\n\nWe minimize C\u2085 subject to constraints using the Adam optimizer on the gradient of the overlap integral. The projection operator ensures feasibility after each update.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialization\n- Set n_points = 200, dx = 2.0 / n_points\n- If initial_h_values available, interpolate to 200 points\n- Initialize h = initial_h_values / sum(initial_h_values) * (n_points/2)\n- Initialize Adam optimizer parameters (\u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999, \u03b5=1e-7)\n\nb. Optimization Loop\nFor up to budget_s seconds:\n1. Compute correlation = np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode='full')\n2. Compute C\u2085 = max(correlation) * dx\n3. Compute gradient:\n   - For each i, compute finite difference (h[i] \u00b1 \u03b5)\n   - Scale by (1 - h[i+k]) contributions\n4. Update h using Adam optimizer\n5. Project h to feasible region:\n   - Clip h to [0,1]\n   - Scale h to maintain \u2211h = n_points/2\n6. Apply simulated annealing perturbation with probability decreasing over time\n\nc. Termination\nStop when time budget exhausted or no improvement in 500 iterations.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Adaptive learning rate: initial \u03b1=0.001\n- Annealing schedule: perturbation probability = max(0.1 * exp(-t/100), 0.01)\n- Gradient step size: \u03b5=1e-5\n- Perturbation magnitude: 0.05\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Adam optimizer updates: O(n_points)\n- Correlation computation: O(n_points\u00b2)\n- Projected updates and perturbations: O(n_points)\n- Expected iterations: ~500-1000 within 600s\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure \u2211h = n_points/2 after projection\n- Validate C\u2085 computation matches evaluator\n- Return (h_values.tolist(), C\u2085, n_points)",
